United States gubernatorial elections were held on November 7, 2006, in 36 states and two territories. The elections coincided with the midterm elections of the [[2006 United States Senate elections|United States Senate]] and the [[2006 United States House of Representatives elections|United States House of Representatives]].

Democrats won open Republican-held governorships in [[2006 Arkansas gubernatorial election|Arkansas]], [[2006 Colorado gubernatorial election|Colorado]], [[2006 Massachusetts gubernatorial election|Massachusetts]], [[2006 New York gubernatorial election|New York]], and [[2006 Ohio gubernatorial election|Ohio]]; and they defeated Republican incumbent [[Bob Ehrlich]] in [[2006 Maryland gubernatorial election|Maryland]] while retaining all of their seats, including their lone open seat in [[2006 Iowa gubernatorial election|Iowa]]. Meanwhile, Republicans held open seats in [[2006 Florida gubernatorial election|Florida]], [[2006 Idaho gubernatorial election|Idaho]], and [[2006 Nevada gubernatorial election|Nevada]], as well as [[2006 Alaska gubernatorial election|Alaska]], where incumbent governor [[Frank Murkowski]] was defeated in the primary. Voters in the [[Insular area|United States territories]] of [[Guam]] (then-Republican) and the [[United States Virgin Islands]] (then-Democratic, but term-limited) also chose their governors and voters elected a new mayor for the [[District of Columbia]], the District's chief executive.

As part of the [[2006 United States elections|2006 Democratic sweep]], Democrats did not lose a single incumbent or open seat to the Republicans in any congressional or gubernatorial contest. The results of the 2006 elections gave [[Republican Party (United States)|Republicans]] 22 governors to the [[Democratic Party (United States)|Democrats]]' 28, a reversal of the numbers held by the respective parties prior to the elections. Republicans held the majority of governorships from 1995 until 2007.

As of {{CURRENTYEAR}}, this election marked the last time that the Democratic Party won gubernatorial elections in [[Oklahoma]], [[Tennessee]], or [[Wyoming]]. This is also the very last gubernatorial election cycle in which the Republican Party won governorships in [[California]], [[Connecticut]], [[Hawaii]], [[Minnesota]], and [[Rhode Island]] to date.

