}}The retirement of term-limited Governor [[Bill Owens (Colorado politician)|Bill Owens]] revealed divisions among the state's Republicans. Republican congressman [[Bob Beauprez]], widely regarded as a conservative, was attacked by his primary opponent, former [[University of Denver]] President [[Marc Holtzman]] for compromising with Democrats in Congress. Beauprez became the nominee when Holtzman failed to submit enough valid signatures to qualify for the ballot, but the negative attacks they exchanged damaged Beauprez's campaign. The Democratic nominee was former [[Denver]] District Attorney [[Bill Ritter]], an anti-abortion Catholic and a political centrist who could not easily be portrayed as a liberal. Ritter did, however, support [[Colorado Referendum I (2006)|Referendum I]] and oppose [[Colorado Amendment 43 (2006)|Amendment 43]]; conversely, the public defeated the former and passed the latter. Ritter's campaign was boosted when he was endorsed by a group of [[Larimer County]] Republicans. During the period of January through August, Ritter raised almost twice as much as Beauprez.<ref>{{Cite web |url=http://www.rockymountainnews.com/drmn/elections/article/0,2808,DRMN_24736_4986347,00.html |title = Rocky Mountain News: Elections  |access-date=2006-09-12 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20060920184600/http://www.rockymountainnews.com/drmn/elections/article/0,2808,DRMN_24736_4986347,00.html |archive-date=2006-09-20 |url-status=dead }}</ref> According to an October 16 Zogby poll, Ritter led Beauprez 47% to 45%.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.zogby.com/|title=Home {{pipe}} Zogby Liquor Store + Liquor Shop|website=Zogby}}</ref> An October 22 SurveyUSA poll showed Ritter leading Beauprez by a larger margin, 56% to 38%.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.surveyusa.com/client/PollReportEmail.aspx?g=630c4b2b-b148-433e-b026-d6aaab91af71|title=Results of SurveyUSA Election Poll #10436|website=www.surveyusa.com}}</ref> Similarly, an October 22 Rasmussen Reports poll showed Ritter leading Beauprez, 51% to 39%.<ref>{{Cite web|date=29 October 2006|title=Colorado Governor: Ritter by 12|url=http://www.rasmussenreports.com/2006/State%20Polls/October%202006/ColoradoGovernor.htm|url-status=dead|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20090107162223/http://www.rasmussenreports.com/2006/State%20Polls/October%202006/ColoradoGovernor.htm|archive-date=2009-01-07|access-date=2006-10-29|website=rasmussenreports.com}}</ref>

Democratic nominee Bill Ritter was elected with 57.0% of the vote.

