}}Governor [[Jeb Bush]] was term-limited. [[Florida Attorney General]] [[Charlie Crist]], a moderate, won the Republican primary with 64%, defeating the [[Chief Financial Officer of Florida]], [[Tom Gallagher]], who received only 34%. Congressman [[Jim Davis (Florida politician)|Jim Davis]] of [[Tampa, Florida|Tampa]] won the Democratic primary with 47% of the vote, defeating [[Florida Senate|State Senator]] [[Rod Smith (politician)|Rod Smith]] of [[Alachua, Florida|Alachua]], who received 41% of the vote. In addition to Crist and Davis, [[Reform Party of the United States of America|Reform Party]] nominee [[Max Linn]] also appeared on the ballot in the general election.

Crist came out of the September 12 primary with momentum, but as the election drew closer, polls began to show a more competitive race. An October 23 [[Quinnipiac University Polling Institute|Quinnipiac]] poll October 23 showed Crist's lead down to 2%.<ref>{{Cite web|date=23 October 2006|title=Democrat Almost Catches Crist In Florida Gov Race, Quinnipiac University Poll Finds; Big Shift By Independent Voters To Davis|url=https://poll.qu.edu/Poll-Release-Legacy?releaseid=975|url-status=live|access-date=2021-07-20|website=poll.qu.edu|publisher=Quinnipiac University|language=en|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210720230620/https://poll.qu.edu/Poll-Release-Legacy?releaseid=975 |archive-date=2021-07-20 }}</ref> However, an October 26 [[Rasmussen Reports]] poll had Crist leading Davis 52% to 41%.<ref>{{Cite web |url=http://www.rasmussenreports.com/2006/State%20Polls/October%202006/FloridaGovernor1026.htm |title=Florida Governor: Crist (R) 52%; Davis (D) 41% |access-date=2006-10-31 |website= www.rasmussenreports.com|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20080704213745/http://www.rasmussenreports.com/2006/State%20Polls/October%202006/FloridaGovernor1026.htm |archive-date=2008-07-04 }}</ref>

Republican nominee Charlie Crist was elected with 52.2% of the vote.

