}}Incumbent Rod Blagojevich had proven to be an incredible fundraiser, and governed a relatively strong [[blue state]]. But recent opinion polling showed that his approval rating at a rather dismal 44%.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.surveyusa.com/client/PollTrack.aspx?g=4b7a9e06-3d07-4716-96a4-40a1bc93b5c2|title=SurveyUSA - IL Gov|website=www.surveyusa.com}}</ref> Blagojevich initially had the advantage in the general election, leading his Republican challenger, state Treasurer [[Judy Baar Topinka]] by eight percentage points in polls, although not reaching the fifty percent "safe zone" for incumbents. In March, Topinka won the GOP primary by 38% to 32% over dairy magnate [[Jim Oberweis]].  Meanwhile, a former [[Chicago]] Alderman named Edwin Eisendrath won a surprising 30% in the Democratic primary. During the election [[United States Attorney]] [[Patrick Fitzgerald]] was looking into the hiring practices of Governor Blagojevich.<ref>{{Cite web|last=Wills|first=Christopher|date=1 July 2006|title=Feds Probing Illinois Gov. Office Hiring|url=http://www.cbsnews.com/stories/2006/07/01/ap/politics/mainD8IISTH00.shtml|url-status=dead|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20071224194400/http://www.cbsnews.com/stories/2006/07/01/ap/politics/mainD8IISTH00.shtml|archive-date=2007-12-24|website=cbsnews.com}}</ref>

An October 15 Rasmussen Reports poll showed Blagojevich dropping 4 points, to end with 44% and Topinka staying at 36%.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.rasmussenreports.com/2006/State%20Polls/October%202006/IllinoisGovernor.htm |title = Election Polls 2006: Illinois Governor  |access-date=October 20, 2006 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20061020003643/https://www.rasmussenreports.com/2006/State%20Polls/October%202006/IllinoisGovernor.htm |archive-date=October 20, 2006 }}</ref> An October 22 SurveyUSA poll had Blagojevich leading Topinka 44% to 34% with 8% undecided.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.surveyusa.com/client/PollReportEmail.aspx?g=40acf414-2c7d-4e40-bf16-d249fb9a090d|title=Results of SurveyUSA Election Poll #10435|website=www.surveyusa.com}}</ref> However, an October 31 Mason-Dixon poll showed Blagojevich leading Topinka only 44% to 40% with 9% undecided.

Democratic incumbent [[Rod Blagojevich]] was re-elected. [[Green Party (United States)|Green Party]] candidate Rich Whitney showed one of the best showings of a third-party candidate in the 2006 election. Whitney received 361,336 votes, or 10% of the ballot share. This made the Green party an official major party in the state of Illinois.

