Polls consistently showed Baldacci with a small lead.  An October 17 Rasmussen Reports poll had Baldacci with 44% and Woodcock at 34%.<ref>{{Cite web |url=http://www.rasmussenreports.com/2006/State%20Polls/October%202006/MaineGovernor.htm |title = Election Polls 2006: Maine Governor  |access-date=2006-10-20 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20061020003657/https://www.rasmussenreports.com/2006/State%20Polls/October%202006/MaineGovernor.htm |archive-date=2006-10-20 |url-status=dead }}</ref>  Meanwhile, a [http://www.wcsh6.com/news/article.aspx?storyid=44615 Voice of the Voter poll]{{Dead link|date=April 2019 |bot=InternetArchiveBot |fix-attempted=yes }} announced by [[WCSH]] on November 6, one day before the election, gave [[John Baldacci]] his smallest lead yet with only 36%, with Senator [[Chandler Woodcock]] 30% and the now leading independent [[Barbara Merrill]] 22%, more than doubling her share. [[Maine Green Independent Party|Green Independent]] candidate [[Pat LaMarche]] polled at 11%.

Baldacci was reelected with 38% of the vote compared to Woodcock's 30%, with 21.55% going to independent Barbara Merrill.

