A November 2 SurveyUSA poll had O'Malley leading Ehrlich 48% to 47% with 2% undecided.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.surveyusa.com/client/PollReportEmail.aspx?g=270b37a5-9804-4f4d-87c6-e61e82103063|title=Results of SurveyUSA Election Poll #10623|website=www.surveyusa.com}}</ref> A November 3 Mason-Dixon poll has O'Malley and Ehrlich tied at 45% with 9% undecided.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.nbcnews.com/id/wbna15547440|title=Poll: Maryland|website=NBC News}}</ref> Democratic nominee [[Martin O'Malley]] was elected.

When Ehrlich unexpectedly beat his Democratic challenger, Lt. Governor [[Kathleen Kennedy Townsend|Kathleen Kennedy]] in 2002, and became first Republican [[Governor of Maryland]] since [[Spiro T. Agnew]], he was regarded by many as potential presidential candidate for [[2008 United States presidential election|2008]].

O'Malley defeated Ehrlich in the general election, 52.7% to 46.2%.

