}}With his approval ratings down, Governor [[Mitt Romney]] opted not to seek a second term. Romney endorsed his [[Lieutenant Governor of Massachusetts|lieutenant governor]], [[Kerry Healey]], in her bid to succeed him. Healey was unopposed in the Republican primary. [[Deval Patrick]], a former [[United States Assistant Attorney General|U.S. Assistant Attorney General]] who headed the [[United States Department of Justice|Department of Justice]]'s [[United States Department of Justice Civil Rights Division|Civil Rights Division]], won the Democratic primary with 50% of the vote<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://archive.boston.com/news/special/politics/2006_elections/primary_results/|title=2006 Massachusetts Primary Election Results - Boston Globe - Boston.com|website=archive.boston.com}}</ref> against [[Thomas Reilly (Massachusetts politician)|Thomas Reilly]] and [[Chris Gabrieli]]. Third-party candidates included [[Grace Ross]] of the [[Green-Rainbow Party]] and independent [[Christy Mihos]], a former Republican and board member on the state Turnpike Authority. Over the course of the campaign, Patrick was the victim of several smears by the Healey campaign, including reports of his brother-in-law's criminal history that were leaked to the press.

On November 7, Deval Patrick was elected with 56% of the vote. He became the first African American governor ever elected in the history of the state, and just the second in the nation's history (the first was [[Douglas Wilder]], a Democrat from Virginia, who served as [[Governor of Virginia]] from 1990 to 1994). Patrick was also the first Democratic governor of Massachusetts since [[Michael Dukakis]] left office in 1991.

