| title = Governor
| before_election = [[Bob Taft]]
| before_party = Republican Party (United States)
| after_election = [[Ted Strickland]]
| after_party = Democratic Party (United States)
}}Term-limited incumbent governor [[Bob Taft]] was viewed as one of the most unpopular governors in the history of [[Ohio]]. Polls showed his approval rating in the vicinity of 10% to 25%.
Congressman [[Ted Strickland]] won the Democratic primary with 79% of the vote, defeating [[Ohio House of Representatives|state representative]] Bryan Flannery. The Republican primary, between [[Ohio Secretary of State]] [[Ken Blackwell]] and [[Ohio Attorney General]] [[Jim Petro]], was more competitive by far. Petro came under fire for switching positions on same-sex marriage and abortion, as well as allegedly taking business from lawyers who refused to give him campaign contributions.<ref>{{Cite web|last=Skolnick|first=David|date=10 April 2006|title=Petro blasts Blackwell, his ideas|url=http://www.vindy.com/news/2006/apr/10/petro-blasts-blackwell-his-ideas/|url-status=dead|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190702235243/http://www.vindy.com/news/2006/apr/10/petro-blasts-blackwell-his-ideas/|archive-date=2019-07-02|website=vindy.com}}</ref> Blackwell and Petro also split over proposals to reduce state spending. Blackwell ultimately won the primary with 56% of the vote.

Blackwell was not a close ally of disgraced Governor Taft, but Taft's unpopularity still damaged his campaign. The negativity of the Republican primary also damaged Blackwell's general election campaign.  In addition, in 2006 there was a nationwide trend towards the Democratic Party.  An October 6 poll by Rasmussen Reports showed that Strickland led by 52% to 40%, a decline from September.<ref>{{Cite web |url=http://www.rasmussenreports.com/2006/State%20Polls/October%202006/OhioGovernor.htm |title = Election Poll 2006: Ohio Governor  |access-date=2006-10-15 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20061012041844/http://www.rasmussenreports.com/2006/State%20Polls/October%202006/OhioGovernor.htm |archive-date=2006-10-12 |url-status=dead }}</ref>  By contrast, an October 12 SurveyUSA poll had Strickland leading Blackwell 60% to 32%.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.surveyusa.com/client/PollReport.aspx?g=b6981613-3f0e-466d-811c-cfa9a6c1111b|title=SurveyUSA Election Poll #10447|website=www.surveyusa.com}}</ref>

Democratic nominee Ted Strickland was elected with 60.5% of the vote. He became the first Democratic [[Governor of Ohio]] since [[Dick Celeste]].

